WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.537
Pivot tables provide a spreadsheet-like way to summarize numeric values across categories.

00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:12.772
In the example, a pivot table compares revenue using channel and order status.

00:00:12.772 --> 00:00:18.342
This helps us see how a measure changes across two categorical dimensions.

00:00:18.342 --> 00:00:21.022
A cross tab is slightly different.

00:00:21.022 --> 00:00:24.157
It counts combinations of categories.

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:30.218
For example, it can show how many completed and cancelled orders occurred in each channel.

00:00:30.801 --> 00:00:35.893
These tools are useful when you want a compact summary that can be inspected quickly.

00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:42.123
The main difference to remember is that a pivot table usually summarizes a numeric measure,

00:00:42.123 --> 00:00:45.799
while a cross tab is commonly used for category counts.
